sec.p:
This section includes the unprocessed, unsegmented EEG data recorded for each patient throughout the entire experiment. The ‘.set’ and ‘.fdt’ files store meta information and raw data respectively. These files can be loaded using the EEGLAB plugin to obtain an ‘EEG’ structure variable, which contains the raw data formatted as ‘channels × time-samples’, channel information, sampling rate, label information, etc. The naming convention is consistent with that of the preprocessed data, as shown below:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$${sub}-{xx}{\rm{\_}}{group}{\rm{\_}}s{ession}{\rm{\_}}{yy}{\rm{\_}}{original}.{set}/.{fdt}$$\end{document}sub−xx_group_session_yy_original.set/.fdtwhere ‘xx’ is the subject ID, ‘group’ is the intervention group received for patients (B represents BCI group, T represents Traditional group), ‘session’ indicates the collection phases (pre represents the pre-training phase, post represents the post-training phase, follow represents the follow-up phase), and ‘yy’ is the run number.

Raw data were preprocessed using the EEGLAB toolbox based on MATLAB and saved in ‘.mat’ format. These files can be loaded directly through MATLAB to obtain an ‘EEG’ structure that contains the preprocessed data and meta information. The files are named according to the following convention:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$${sub}-{xx}{\rm{\_}}{group}{\rm{\_}}s{ession}{\rm{\_}}{yy}{\rm{\_}}{eeg}.{mat}$$\end{document}sub−xx_group_session_yy_eeg.matwhere ‘xx’ is the subject ID, ‘group’ is the intervention group received for patients (B represents BCI group, T represents Traditional group), ‘session’ indicates the collection phases (pre represents the pre-training phase, post represents the post-training phase, follow represents the follow-up phase), and ‘yy’ is the run number.

The ERD23 phenomenon is the main brain activation index for MI-BCI. event-related spectral perturbation (ERSP) was used to reflect the event-related power variations of the induced EEG signals in time and frequency domain24, which defined as follows:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$${ERSP}\left. (f,t\right)=\frac{1}{n}\mathop{\sum }\limits_{k=1}^{n}({F}_{k}{(f,t)}^{2})$$\end{document}ERSPf,t=1n∑k=1n(Fk(f,t)2)where n is the number of trials, and \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$${F}_{k}{(f,t)}^{2}$$\end{document}Fk(f,t)2 is the spectral estimation of kth trial at frequency f and time t. And in order to quantify the ERD phenomenon, the baseline correction was conducted by subtracting the average ERSP value within the baseline period (1 s before the start of the MI task), which defined as follows:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
